#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175832 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175982 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb9cfe3a-6341-5a25-a513-8ef20b5a7f7c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Separable Form by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "acac66ab-ff2a-5727-8a2c-9cce3e18806c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9036" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2xy - x^2 - y^2.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z= x - y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z'(x) = f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-(x-y)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x - y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 - \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  1 - \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  =  z^2 + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z) = z^2 + 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175833 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175983 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6655eb29-8410-5a5c-be86-457affcfb4c3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "37a41ca0-2205-5368-91ec-d9a4c1a309d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9038" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\dfrac{1 - x - y}{x + y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = 2x + C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1-(x+y)}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1 + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{1-z}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int z\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{2} & =x+K \\\\ {z}^{2} & =2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = 2K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+y)^2 = 2x + C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y) = (x+y)^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175983 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6655eb29-8410-5a5c-be86-457affcfb4c3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce3a1b4-a3cb-50bf-b683-6273efe59b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x-y+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241424} {:answer/id #uuid "550dce1b-1d12-5c00-bf63-e187f3cc82c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241946} {:answer/id #uuid "64af1c39-4794-55cb-9588-01a284e589ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242084} {:answer/id #uuid "78a345f7-f9a1-528b-92c0-afaefd79fa28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242242} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f387dc-ce71-5141-93b8-e3126bdabe76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78a345f7-f9a1-528b-92c0-afaefd79fa28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242242} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6fa166b-2a28-5db4-b15a-93030703eeb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0db4ae1-7ca8-5d44-90b5-79a2a42ad1ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-107815" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x+y}{3-2x-y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=-\\frac{x+y}{3}+C\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x+y}{3-(2x+y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\frac{z}{3-z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6-z}{3-z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{3-z}{6-z})\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int (\\frac{z-3}{z-6})\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (\\frac{3}{z-6}+1)\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|z-6|+z & =x+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+y-6|+2x+y & =x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+y-6| & =-\\frac{x+y}{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{K}{3}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=2x+y-6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175982 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb9cfe3a-6341-5a25-a513-8ef20b5a7f7c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Separable Form by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32c80b26-7f38-5a97-830c-d06b34e08ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2z+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241628} {:answer/id #uuid "529cd17c-ec27-51f2-a416-e57083e84271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241915} {:answer/id #uuid "d367861f-20c2-5579-862d-4a172b336d6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6z+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243046} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb82926-337e-5ea5-8f9c-b0390f0da92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243298} {:answer/id #uuid "fa247d30-5b7a-5eab-a0cd-227b4d08a807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3z+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d367861f-20c2-5579-862d-4a172b336d6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6z+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243046} :answer-field/id #uuid "20095377-c06c-59ec-bd4f-65eaa1b42e8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05430704-5a00-5ff9-8973-3229343f5e50" :question/math-academy-id "q-108735" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3x+6y.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+2y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3(x+2y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1) & =3z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=6z+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=6z+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175982 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb9cfe3a-6341-5a25-a513-8ef20b5a7f7c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Separable Form by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0805556b-de15-5750-9efc-2cad705e96b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2z+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241247} {:answer/id #uuid "16a668da-07f5-57a0-8797-6d731ca2bb90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4z+1}{6z+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241375} {:answer/id #uuid "37b6092c-7155-536f-931f-d472df430d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241673} {:answer/id #uuid "9391a889-745a-5115-a7d7-cb17455f64d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{z+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242473} {:answer/id #uuid "990c2541-db1b-5c93-a31c-2ea2a1641106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{z+1}{z+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0805556b-de15-5750-9efc-2cad705e96b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2z+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241247} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5fa1c74-bebe-573e-99bc-a11d6c3f095e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "463a8e36-eaf0-59f2-9877-1d9a9eb456ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-108748" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{8x-12y+1}{12x-18y+3}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=2x-3y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4(2x-3y)+1}{6(2x-3y)+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-3y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-3\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}(2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{4z+1}{6z+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2z+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{1}{2z+1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175982 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb9cfe3a-6341-5a25-a513-8ef20b5a7f7c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Separable Form by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "001a774c-7693-538c-8b59-09b4f387a073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241186} {:answer/id #uuid "18a5bb0a-c2f3-5bde-9bf1-5b9d33650526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{z+2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241393} {:answer/id #uuid "5042ecec-8ae8-5bc7-a301-d9dbf98f00f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241895} {:answer/id #uuid "597b5bd9-11c5-522a-9abb-a34c633cf503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241987} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2b7676-5676-5136-9d53-d5c8