"Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Separable Form by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "309c726d-2cd5-537b-a61e-42eb045fdb36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241614} {:answer/id #uuid "65aea346-1778-5f46-89ed-3f890222b933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2z-3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242094} {:answer/id #uuid "7414e3b7-38e2-59d0-906a-de813e37b9cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{z}{z+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242209} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb65ac5-191e-5532-b676-3f5738877285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-z}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242847} {:answer/id #uuid "c20788e3-ad95-53e7-b3b2-7c79a0377e8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2z}{3(z+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "309c726d-2cd5-537b-a61e-42eb045fdb36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241614} :answer-field/id #uuid "68e28cab-16e8-51bd-9e93-1d4234696dc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52822c39-606a-5a54-9e92-3a8ac02903e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-108751" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2+y-x}{x-y}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x-y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2-(x-y)}{x-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2-z}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175983 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6655eb29-8410-5a5c-be86-457affcfb4c3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "608b3d03-19b5-5374-a0fc-a3e27b90ee08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242047} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac8bd69-61c9-5857-b202-82dc6129daa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+3y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242405} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf2b77c-254e-52e0-94a6-cd014f0b7106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242420} {:answer/id #uuid "8c940178-b04a-5b7f-a292-4e37eb8625a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242421} {:answer/id #uuid "961a895c-1016-54fe-b41b-3219981caf71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c940178-b04a-5b7f-a292-4e37eb8625a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242421} :answer-field/id #uuid "3871dbc7-9d0f-5041-a42a-8c541e698f04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b7b4d1c-df4d-5986-bef2-30931183e55d" :question/math-academy-id "q-108780" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2{sin}^{2}⁡(3x+3y)-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(g(x,y))=-6x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(3(x+y))-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(3z)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2{sin}^{2}⁡(3z).\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{csc}^{2}⁡(3z)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\int {csc}^{2}⁡(3z)\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3z) & =2x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡(3z) & =-6x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-3K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+3y)=-6x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=3x+3y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175983 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6655eb29-8410-5a5c-be86-457affcfb4c3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "533a6a94-d3c8-5ff4-94aa-c08ef84b3082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241924} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e329da-d49e-5e18-a430-53cc1128e645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x+5y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242771} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e18b8a-4a64-50af-8e88-559d97acb595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+5y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242866} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e426f6-3ea5-54bd-9758-63f0641b35fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(5x+5y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242914} {:answer/id #uuid "c556433f-db11-5f61-8210-ee22f4bce4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x+5y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "533a6a94-d3c8-5ff4-94aa-c08ef84b3082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241924} :answer-field/id #uuid "e592fca1-0175-5fcf-92d8-6643cf8b3eff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e58752d6-0044-55ba-a578-6076003cbc1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-108819" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+5y)-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=10x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5(x+y))-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5z)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5z).\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5z)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡(5z)\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5z)| & =2x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5z)| & =10x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y)|=10x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175982 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb9cfe3a-6341-5a25-a513-8ef20b5a7f7c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Separable Form by Factoring and Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fd4f187-0154-57a7-9de9-e48447827a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4z+5}{z+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272669} {:answer/id #uuid "601bbc48-fe4b-51b0-95f6-9995c29db302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2z+1}{z+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273214} {:answer/id #uuid "682042fe-d091-55bf-a05e-b4c3e7d30f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-5z}{z+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273286} {:answer/id #uuid "8fd8bb16-bf94-57ed-b66d-b43ee09b8561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2z}{z+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273638} {:answer/id #uuid "928a69db-b58f-519d-938c-e620fd516e8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7z+5}{z+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "928a69db-b58f-519d-938c-e620fd516e8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7z+5}{z+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273660} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcd9d89c-8249-5a15-9f45-7cb59bbc1f85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fd5b90f-0ac6-584e-ad1f-769d139ad924" :question/math-academy-id "q-149744" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{10x+2y}{5x+y+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=5x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2(5x+y)}{(5x+y)+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=5x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-5 & =\\frac{2z}{z+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{7z+5}{z+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{7z+5}{z+1}.$"}]}
